<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franc of Bruges</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Liberty of Bruges, Brugse Vrije</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franc of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> was the rural castellany surrounding Bruges in the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, which governed itself through its own aldermen. Prosperous and populous, it was recognised in the fourteenth century as the fourth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-members-of-flanders">Four Members of Flanders</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, Bruges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>. It lasted until the French annexation of 1795, and its palace still stands on the Burg square in Bruges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franc-of-bruges.jpg" alt="Franc of Bruges" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem Blaeu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrugseVrije1664.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
